[[:db/retract 17592186167256 :question/answer-fields 17592186164695] [:db/retract 17592186167256 :question/answer-fields 17592186164696] {:db/id 17592186166922 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7c2a30e4-7147-5605-9ca8-d3892b9005ab" "question-b6c858ce-3897-5421-9543-5d24a7c86dfd" "question-c9a5577e-11c5-5655-a180-370cf60950eb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166557 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-8x+2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=4y-xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Provide an interpretation of each equation in the model." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a rate of change.\n\nFor the $x'$ equation, we have\n\n$$\nx' = -8x + 2xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the predator. Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-8x$ term), and\n\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $2xy$ term).\n\nFor the $y'$ equation, we have\n\n$$\ny' = 4y - xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$ must represent the prey. Indeed, \n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $4y$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-xy$ term)." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166923 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-54015625-9f74-5e9e-ac7d-555ab045f6d5" "question-deb74bf3-ea72-561b-8b82-34ede161ce61"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166554 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+7xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=9y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model." :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy=0 \\\\ 9y-5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy & =0 \\\\ x(-4+7y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\dfrac{4}{7}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\dfrac{4}{7},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-5xy & =0 \\\\ y(9-5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}(9-5x) & =0 \\\\ 9-5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{7}\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$\\left( \\dfrac{9}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{7} \\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166924 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8df5273a-07e4-5ba8-bfd6-b4b9ed059597" "question-3850fcb7-fc55-55b2-8f9f-750fe16ba10c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166555 :question/problem "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-0.4x+0.4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=y-4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.25, \\, 1]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=-0.4x+0.4xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=y-4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.4x+0.4xy)=-0.4+0.4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.4x+0.4xy)=0.4x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-4xy)=-4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-4xy)=1-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/1f/1fe3902e5e6617b35b590ff7084996c58a9847a7fe5fe44ee3c11b43b44eb0b7.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/f1/f1ada7cc81f4ca1223daa261ef7e938e8ed8ccf1c2e02a4e167a6f8dcecad1dd.svg) to get\n\n![](images/f0/f008d0f12f586562736b812660ae0b64b0656df1b6509ecc5125f3427b2df245.svg)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0.1 \\\\ -4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-(-4)\\cdot 0.1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm 0.2\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is a stable center.\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source)." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166925 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ac25cc32-c574-57e4-8147-410ae2ef0848"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166556 :question/problem "Consider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-10x+5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=21y-7xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t), y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nDescribe the trajectory and the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n![](images/86/86002a25d2c60e07c34976541a1c050e606d20e4ffa7928a44f940bf5fa736e3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-ax+bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=cy-dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\nWith that in mind, let's describe the system:\n\n- The trajectory circles around the nonzero equilibrium.\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey decreases and the number of predators increases." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167250 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=6y-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${y}^{′}$ in the second equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${y}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6y-2xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$ must represent the $\\text{prey}.$ Indeed, \n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $6y$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-2xy$ term)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164617 :answer-field/correct 17592186164618 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164614 :answer-field/correct 17592186164615 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-7c2a30e4-7147-5605-9ca8-d3892b9005ab" :question/id #uuid "7c2a30e4-7147-5605-9ca8-d3892b9005ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-334986" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2.5x+3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=4.5y-1.5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${x}^{′}$ in the first equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${x}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=-2.5x+3xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the $\\text{predator}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-2.5x$ term), and\n\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $3xy$ term)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8bdcfa39-d426-5f32-aadc-e305d4d7db21" "answer-field-e62a6bf9-30b3-5c9e-ab14-91175aa58de8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8bdcfa39-d426-5f32-aadc-e305d4d7db21" :answer-field/id #uuid "8bdcfa39-d426-5f32-aadc-e305d4d7db21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-6e4899d3-ec39-5328-b6b4-ec29e3ec2902" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe1b4bdf-e9c1-5d5b-acee-d33c7f800149" "answer-6e4899d3-ec39-5328-b6b4-ec29e3ec2902"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe1b4bdf-e9c1-5d5b-acee-d33c7f800149" :answer/id #uuid "fe1b4bdf-e9c1-5d5b-acee-d33c7f800149" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "amount" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e4899d3-ec39-5328-b6b4-ec29e3ec2902" :answer/id #uuid "6e4899d3-ec39-5328-b6b4-ec29e3ec2902" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "rate of change" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e62a6bf9-30b3-5c9e-ab14-91175aa58de8" :answer-field/id #uuid "e62a6bf9-30b3-5c9e-ab14-91175aa58de8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8c98e75b-1b50-5f02-974a-29c12fa13e44" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8c98e75b-1b50-5f02-974a-29c12fa13e44" "answer-aa59b7af-054a-5639-bbba-7a80a086495c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c98e75b-1b50-5f02-974a-29c12fa13e44" :answer/id #uuid "8c98e75b-1b50-5f02-974a-29c12fa13e44" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "predator" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa59b7af-054a-5639-bbba-7a80a086495c" :answer/id #uuid "aa59b7af-054a-5639-bbba-7a80a086495c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "prey" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b6c858ce-3897-5421-9543-5d24a7c86dfd" :question/id #uuid "b6c858ce-3897-5421-9543-5d24a7c86dfd" :question/math-academy-id "q-334991" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=6x-0.9xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3.2y+2.4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${y}^{′}$ in the second equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${y}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3.2y+2.4xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$ must represent the $\\text{predator}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-3.2y$ term), and\n\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $2.4xy$ term)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0385b0de-5182-56de-87fe-bd21709d14e6" "answer-field-4a3e6e3a-0204-57e7-bf52-d34b7b48737e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0385b0de-5182-56de-87fe-bd21709d14e6" :answer-field/id #uuid "0385b0de-5182-56de-87fe-bd21709d14e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-10bc4a5f-cfa8-5e4e-a6dd-8a34056a0b6b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-32668252-978b-5bc2-9826-63d40da6593b" "answer-10bc4a5f-cfa8-5e4e-a6dd