t the $\\text{prey}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $2.5x$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-0.5xy$ term)." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c9a5577e-11c5-5655-a180-370cf60950eb" :question/id #uuid "c9a5577e-11c5-5655-a180-370cf60950eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-334984" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3y+1.2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Select the correct options in the following statement.\n\n$\\,$\nTerm ${x}^{′}$ in the first equation represents the \n{{field-1}} \nof the \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a $\\text{rate of change}.$\n\nFor the ${x}^{′}$ equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=4x-2xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the $\\text{prey}.$ Indeed,\n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $4x$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-2xy$ term)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ab642a1c-0625-5e88-acf1-2a123c709861" "answer-field-e647fbdf-2c89-5dcd-bdc8-ddd3b359bf27"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ab642a1c-0625-5e88-acf1-2a123c709861" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab642a1c-0625-5e88-acf1-2a123c709861" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-b3e928f0-0f11-57d6-be00-5c4143504482" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b17487cf-7a52-5d9a-ba7c-f5b133431e84" "answer-b3e928f0-0f11-57d6-be00-5c4143504482"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b17487cf-7a52-5d9a-ba7c-f5b133431e84" :answer/id #uuid "b17487cf-7a52-5d9a-ba7c-f5b133431e84" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "amount" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3e928f0-0f11-57d6-be00-5c4143504482" :answer/id #uuid "b3e928f0-0f11-57d6-be00-5c4143504482" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "rate of change" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e647fbdf-2c89-5dcd-bdc8-ddd3b359bf27" :answer-field/id #uuid "e647fbdf-2c89-5dcd-bdc8-ddd3b359bf27" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-0145e0a8-76fe-591b-b9ca-09fd7e8080ff" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd65ef92-00c8-5fe6-8d79-beb9d3f6d86b" "answer-0145e0a8-76fe-591b-b9ca-09fd7e8080ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd65ef92-00c8-5fe6-8d79-beb9d3f6d86b" :answer/id #uuid "bd65ef92-00c8-5fe6-8d79-beb9d3f6d86b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "predator" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0145e0a8-76fe-591b-b9ca-09fd7e8080ff" :answer/id #uuid "0145e0a8-76fe-591b-b9ca-09fd7e8080ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "prey" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-54015625-9f74-5e9e-ac7d-555ab045f6d5" :question/id #uuid "54015625-9f74-5e9e-ac7d-555ab045f6d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335003" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=7x-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7x-4xy=0 \\\\ -6y+3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-4xy & =0 \\\\ x(7-4y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{7}{4}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{7}{4},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+3x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}(-6+3x) & =0 \\\\ -6+3x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(2,\\frac{7}{4}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aebfc45b-de88-5aad-aeaf-288e839a09f1" "answer-field-fc175313-c1cd-5ef5-81ac-641814b985cd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aebfc45b-de88-5aad-aeaf-288e839a09f1" :answer-field/id #uuid "aebfc45b-de88-5aad-aeaf-288e839a09f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f05771b2-5acd-5c74-bcf1-b8647b5a5a5a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f05771b2-5acd-5c74-bcf1-b8647b5a5a5a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f05771b2-5acd-5c74-bcf1-b8647b5a5a5a" :answer/id #uuid "f05771b2-5acd-5c74-bcf1-b8647b5a5a5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc175313-c1cd-5ef5-81ac-641814b985cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc175313-c1cd-5ef5-81ac-641814b985cd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d2759ddb-1657-5331-8039-4a80dc4d88e6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2759ddb-1657-5331-8039-4a80dc4d88e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2759ddb-1657-5331-8039-4a80dc4d88e6" :answer/id #uuid "d2759ddb-1657-5331-8039-4a80dc4d88e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167256 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x-2xy=0 \\\\ -4y+5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2xy & =0 \\\\ x(3-2y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{3}{2},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+5xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(-4+5x) & =0 \\\\ -4+5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{2}).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186370196 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2x+5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=7y-3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+5xy=0 \\\\ 7y-3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+5xy & =0 \\\\ x(-2+5y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-3(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{2}{5},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-3xy & =0 \\\\ y(7-3x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(7-3x) & =0 \\\\ 7-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{2}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{2}{5}).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-deb74bf3-ea72-561b-8b82-34ede161ce61" :question/id #uuid "deb74bf3-ea72-561b-8b82-34ede161ce61" :question/math-academy-id "q-334996" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+7xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=6y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy=0 \\\\ 6y-5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy & =0 \\\\ x(-4+7y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{4}{7}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y-5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{4}{7},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y-5xy & =0 \\\\ y(6-5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}(6-5x) & =0 \\\\ 6-5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{6}{5},\\frac{4}{7}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{6}{5},\\frac{4}{7}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a1ce7209-c7d2-5426-8c47-a6db5339bfa4" "answer-field-e8c8fb6d-38bf-50ae-8c71-d42b9e1d88c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a1ce7209-c7d2-5426-8c47-a6db5339bfa4" :answer-field/id #uuid "a1ce7209-c7d2-5426-8c47-a6db5339bfa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-223bb1ef-6abd-5b8f-9cc4-9705282c9116" :answer-field/choices ["answer-223bb1ef-6abd-5b8f-9cc4-9705282c9116"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-223bb1ef-6abd-5b8f-9cc4-9705282c9116" :answer/id #uuid "223bb1ef-6abd-5b8f-9cc4-9705282c9116" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e8c8fb6d-38bf-50ae-8c71-d42b9e1d88c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8c8fb6d-38bf-50ae-8c71-d42b9e1d88c2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4042d52e-23f4-5dc8-b1f7-bbe39de58e54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4042d52e-23f4-5dc8-b1f7-bbe39de58e54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4042d52e-23f4-5dc8-b1f7-bbe39de58e54" :answer/id #uuid "4042d52e-23f4-5dc8-b1f7-bbe39de58e54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186370191 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-5x+4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=9y-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x+4xy=0 \\\\ 9y-2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5x+4xy & =0 \\\\ x(-5+4y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{5}{4}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-2(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{5}{4},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-2xy & =0 \\\\ y(9-2x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}(9-2x) & =0 \\\\ 9-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{9}{2},\\frac{5}{4}).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-8df5273a-07e4-5ba8-bfd6-b4b9ed059597" :question/id #uuid "8df5273a-07e4-5ba8-bfd6-b4b9ed059597" :question/math-academy-id "q-335011" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+6xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=7y-3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-4x+6xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=7y-3xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+6xy)=-4+6y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+6xy)=6x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-3xy)=-3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7y-3xy)=7-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}-4+6y & 6x \\\\ -3y & 7-3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}-4+6(0) & 6(0) \\\\ -3(0) & 7-3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=7.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b0abcaff-c4b8-5e20-a1d9-9b29427c8bf3" "answer-field-81fe763c-a0aa-5d05-ba4b-98451dacbbe0" "answer-field-ed7c7de6-3bf0-51f6-9d58-4a88df7f061e" "answer-field-8027076d-da39-55d8-8905-34f2c92598fb" "answer-field-522a538a-7746-58cb-a659-f1c4f5e01ebf" "answer-field-1431c518-0720-5741-b2e4-d9808709c654" "answer-field-b58ed4e5-6c54-5acb-ad76-d1a197c4b886"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b0abcaff-c4b8-5e20-a1d9-9b29427c8bf3" :answer-field/id #uuid "b0abcaff-c4b8-5e20-a1d9-9b29427c8bf3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-e137be6f-aaa0-5712-9d3d-9393aa99d400" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5dd6af90-2305-5b03-99b1-11cd5890f63b" "answer-e137be6f-aaa0-5712-9d3d-9393aa99d400" "answer-d2a3d13b-0d18-5441-89cf-f2943256ceca" "answer-48b37648-8efd-532a-a3b2-486d560f90d9" "answer-fec173dc-889d-510c-af42-317818648da1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dd6af90-2305-5b03-99b1-11cd5890f63b" :answer/id #uuid "5dd6af90-2305-5b03-99b1-11cd5890f63b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e137be6f-aaa0-5712-9d3d-9393aa99d400" :answer/id #uuid "