03f641b-55a6-5ab2-9892-aba7070b3f94" :answer-field/id #uuid "a03f641b-55a6-5ab2-9892-aba7070b3f94" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8ad13af8-2298-5820-a02d-ffb16c047ece" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8ad13af8-2298-5820-a02d-ffb16c047ece" "answer-b0a0d9ff-d684-540c-860f-b0b91e7cc40c" "answer-165e50cb-c437-5f01-a7b2-19e78e2dd0ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ad13af8-2298-5820-a02d-ffb16c047ece" :answer/id #uuid "8ad13af8-2298-5820-a02d-ffb16c047ece" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0a0d9ff-d684-540c-860f-b0b91e7cc40c" :answer/id #uuid "b0a0d9ff-d684-540c-860f-b0b91e7cc40c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an asymptotically stable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-165e50cb-c437-5f01-a7b2-19e78e2dd0ad" :answer/id #uuid "165e50cb-c437-5f01-a7b2-19e78e2dd0ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an unstable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7c735b62-1fdc-51f8-bd8e-81ae9ffcfbf7" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c735b62-1fdc-51f8-bd8e-81ae9ffcfbf7" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-760580d4-3f22-56e4-a00b-6650cfa2e194" :answer-field/choices ["answer-760580d4-3f22-56e4-a00b-6650cfa2e194" "answer-096339fc-67fa-5587-be24-686f292a7fb6" "answer-0edc2ea7-934e-536b-a572-e448556ea20d" "answer-1dfa7c8c-bce9-55d1-a3bd-a6d5dc251c14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-760580d4-3f22-56e4-a00b-6650cfa2e194" :answer/id #uuid "760580d4-3f22-56e4-a00b-6650cfa2e194" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "center" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-096339fc-67fa-5587-be24-686f292a7fb6" :answer/id #uuid "096339fc-67fa-5587-be24-686f292a7fb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0edc2ea7-934e-536b-a572-e448556ea20d" :answer/id #uuid "0edc2ea7-934e-536b-a572-e448556ea20d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1dfa7c8c-bce9-55d1-a3bd-a6d5dc251c14" :answer/id #uuid "1dfa7c8c-bce9-55d1-a3bd-a6d5dc251c14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186370279 :question/problem "![](images/2a/2aaa545dfada7732569ce6b813f32d050fa453aa83dc101495147d25330b7bc3.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-9y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t),y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n$\\,$\nThe trajectory \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements:\n\n- The trajectory $\\text{circles around}$ $\\text{the nonzero equilibrium}.$\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey $\\text{decreases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{increases}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac25cc32-c574-57e4-8147-410ae2ef0848" :question/id #uuid "ac25cc32-c574-57e4-8147-410ae2ef0848" :question/math-academy-id "q-335046" :question/problem "![](images/67/67cf9112644945b23280f4593ffbd70161651bd9cb389293821f022599ea4a75.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-2x+xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=6y-2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t),y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nSelect the options below that best describe the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n$\\,$\nThe trajectory \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$\nThe number of prey {{field-3}} and the number of predators {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-ax+bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=cy-dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements:\n\n- The trajectory $\\text{circles around}$ $\\text{the nonzero equilibrium}.$\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey $\\text{decreases}$ and the number of predators $\\text{increases}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a024659a-16fb-58ef-8d93-d0ba23795093" "answer-field-e53e60e7-c4f8-56e2-8beb-5790b2749506" "answer-field-34eb5063-5be6-5e26-95ee-63607de5ecbe" "answer-field-d4860970-bcaf-5ca9-a942-b8c587273d02"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a024659a-16fb-58ef-8d93-d0ba23795093" :answer-field/id #uuid "a024659a-16fb-58ef-8d93-d0ba23795093" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-9d7ec0b0-fbd1-5c5a-b0ce-f8d76307a82b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-31492574-3235-5fe2-81bf-2817791b3521" "answer-9d7ec0b0-fbd1-5c5a-b0ce-f8d76307a82b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31492574-3235-5fe2-81bf-2817791b3521" :answer/id #uuid "31492574-3235-5fe2-81bf-2817791b3521" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "approaches" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d7ec0b0-fbd1-5c5a-b0ce-f8d76307a82b" :answer/id #uuid "9d7ec0b0-fbd1-5c5a-b0ce-f8d76307a82b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "circles around" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e53e60e7-c4f8-56e2-8beb-5790b2749506" :answer-field/id #uuid "e53e60e7-c4f8-56e2-8beb-5790b2749506" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-c2c7693e-aad2-5c95-b0d8-4472f77adabc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c2c7693e-aad2-5c95-b0d8-4472f77adabc" "answer-50862e2f-c553-52d0-acde-f30e01204459" "answer-e67694c7-b1db-5b4a-b23b-e0720fa9e835"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2c7693e-aad2-5c95-b0d8-4472f77adabc" :answer/id #uuid "c2c7693e-aad2-5c95-b0d8-4472f77adabc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the nonzero equilibrium" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50862e2f-c553-52d0-acde-f30e01204459" :answer/id #uuid "50862e2f-c553-52d0-acde-f30e01204459" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the origin" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e67694c7-b1db-5b4a-b23b-e0720fa9e835" :answer/id #uuid "e67694c7-b1db-5b4a-b23b-e0720fa9e835" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "the point of inflection" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-34eb5063-5be6-5e26-95ee-63607de5ecbe" :answer-field/id #uuid "34eb5063-5be6-5e26-95ee-63607de5ecbe" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-4e536196-5953-59fb-b1e9-7bde007287af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4e536196-5953-59fb-b1e9-7bde007287af" "answer-c6d7d5eb-2fb2-5cb2-8c07-015b23811f12"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e536196-5953-59fb-b1e9-7bde007287af" :answer/id #uuid "4e536196-5953-59fb-b1e9-7bde007287af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "decreases" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6d7d5eb-2fb2-5cb2-8c07-015b23811f12" :answer/id #uuid "c6d7d5eb-2fb2-5cb2-8c07-015b23811f12" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "increases" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d4860970-bcaf-5ca9-a942-b8c587273d02" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4860970-bcaf-5ca9-a942-b8c587273d02" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-536b4704-9886-5dd7-a4f8-610603bd4048" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6da5d782-8fe0-5ddd-82ea-ca66cae30e79" "answer-536b4704-9886-5dd7-a4f8-610603bd4048"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6da5d782-8fe0-5ddd-82ea-ca66cae30e79" :answer/id #uuid "6da5d782-8fe0-5ddd-82ea-ca66cae30e79" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "decreases" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-536b4704-9886-5dd7-a4f8-610603bd4048" :answer/id #uuid "536b4704-9886-5dd7-a4f8-610603bd4048" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "increases" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168012 :tutorial/content "The **Lotka–Volterra predator-prey model** describes the interaction between two species (predator and prey) and is given by the nonlinear system of the form\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, \n\n- $x=x(t)$ is the *prey population*, and\n\n- $y=y(t)$ is the *predator population*.\n\nRemember that a derivative represents a *rate of change*.\n\n- In the first equation, $x' = ax - bxy,$ the prey changes due to the following:\n\n- The *intrinsic growth*, which is given by the $ax$ term. If there are no predators ($y=0$), then $x' = ax > 0$ and the prey grows exponentially.\n\n- The *predation*, which is given by the $-bxy$ term. When prey and predators interact, the term $-bxy$ decreases $x.$\n\n- In the second equation, $y' = -cy + dxy,$ the predators change due to the following:\n\n- The *intrinsic decay*, which is given by the $-cy$ term. If there is no prey ($x=0$), then $y' = -cy < 0$ and predators decline exponentially.\n\n- The *feeding/reproduction*, which is given by the $dxy$ term. When prey and predators interact, the term $dxy$ increases $y.$\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168026 :tutorial/content "Recall that the equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, for the Lotka–Volterra model \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthe equilibria are the solutions of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}ax-bxy=0 \\\\ -cy+dxy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax-bxy & =0 \\\\ x(a-by) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\dfrac{a}{b}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-cy+d(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\dfrac{a}{b},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-cy+dxy & =0 \\\\ y(-c+dx) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{a}{b}(-c+dx) & =0 \\\\ -c+dx & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{c}{d}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d},\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right).$\n\nTherefore, there are two equilibria:\n\n- The *origin* $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{(0,0)}.$\n\n- The *nonzero equilibrium* $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d},\\,\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right)}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168013 :tutorial/content "Consider the Lotka–Volterra predator-prey model \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants, $x=x(t)$ is the prey population, and $y=y(t)$ is the predator population.\n\nTo classify the equilibrium at the origin, we need to find the corresponding Jacobian matrix.\n\nLet's denote $f_1(x,y)=ax-bxy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-cy+dxy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(ax-bxy)=a-by,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(ax-bxy)=-bx \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-cy+dxy)=dy,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-cy+dxy)=-c+dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/e1/e1c9a7c83c6c5790b264670bba4c8e7352ac18239b737348a85c393b5de3be6e.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/7a/7a3543ed5d9733302a80e87b344d4f74d46d9bc27d5a034692b81cec54a56635.svg) to get\n\n![](images/24/24ac6a3fb192074570c1bf62585a5b8e57e6052b6118159c8f1645a31d92b21f.svg)\n\nSince this matrix is diagonal, the eigenvalues are simply the diagonal entries: $\\lambda_1 = a$ and $\\lambda_2 = -c.$ Because $a,c$ are positive constants, we have $\\lambda_1 > 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168027 :tutorial/content "Next, we evaluate the Jacobian matrix\n![](images/d5/d57e5d0566616dcf983c8d2d9ee8375cb70aa44d4d5933026266396202248d22.svg)\nat the nonzero equilibrium ![](images/b3/b3e4462dae63d3e4cbaddcd890e1553dd26372c2fdca8efe87cbfb8984b9a492.svg) to get\n\n![](images/ec/ecc7fb063630624a8acc4ab201208c2cfb86fe97b910cdc6e17fb71629299385.svg)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -\\frac{bc}{d} \\\\ \\frac{ad}{b} & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )\\cdot (-\\lambda )-(-\\frac{bc}{d})\\cdot \\frac{ad}{b} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+ac & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm \\sqrt{ac}\\,\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nSince the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is a *stable center*.\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model, it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues.\nHowever, we will not go into detail on why in this lesson.\n\nJust keep in mind that for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone would be inconclusive in this case (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source).\n\nTherefore, a typical phase portrait of the system looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/23/236a29ae07d8fd091fd602ad124af53b49754419b949bd42713078bc9d8814b6.png)\n\nThe global picture in the first quadrant is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions which intersect at the saddle point $(0,0){:}$\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach infinity (unstable line). Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey increases exponentially.\n\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin (stable line). Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n\n- The curved trajectories circle around the nonzero equilibrium point $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d},\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168014 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider a typical curved trajectory in more detail.\n\n![](images/73/7330af149428dd84fa1e94fd914a1e71d074f38bfe4ced44928a3c7043ee4fe9.png)\n\nIt can be split into four parts by the lines $x=\\dfrac{c}{d}$ and $y=\\dfrac{a}{b}$ passing through the nonzero equilibrium:\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey *decreases* and the number of predators *increases*.\n\n- From $B$ to $C,$ the number of prey *decreases* and the number of predators *decreases*.\n\n- From $C$ to $D,$ the number of prey *increases* and the number of predators *decreases*.\n\n- From $D$ to $A,$ the number of prey *increases* and the number of predators *increases*.\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/1d/1d04db41fdc9fd8940dec3a130c4dc2a26bd4b3fa3861f40f65b0c708cb16084.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166989 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
